  # ============================================================
# ENGINEERING CONSTITUTION
# xTanBot.ai — AI Voice Assistant Platform
# Stack: React Native · Fastify · Claude API · Twilio Streaming
#        ElevenLabs · BullMQ · Redis · Postgres · Prisma · Turborepo
# ============================================================
# This file is the single source of architectural truth.
# Every generated file, module, and pattern must conform to
# these rules without exception. When in doubt, centralize.
#
# Version:    2.0.0
# Status:     RATIFIED
# Governed by: Staff Architecture Team
# ============================================================


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 0. PRIME DIRECTIVES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

These directives are inviolable. No exception, no workaround, no shortcut.
A pull request that violates any directive below must not be merged.

- Never duplicate logic that already exists in a shared package.
- Never bypass the AI tool router — all Claude invocations go through it.
- Never call external services (Twilio, ElevenLabs, Redis, Prisma) directly
  from route handlers; always go through the service layer.
- Never hardcode secrets, config, or environment values; use @xtanbot/config.
- Never write a service that holds mutable in-memory state.
- Never create a Prisma client outside of @xtanbot/db.
- Never skip Zod validation on any external input boundary.
- Never write unstructured logs; always use @xtanbot/logger.
- Never mix concerns: route → service → repository → infrastructure.
- Never call the Claude API outside of @xtanbot/ai-core.
- Never emit a domain event without a schema defined in @xtanbot/events.
- Never define a prompt string inline in application code; use @xtanbot/ai-core/prompts.
- Never bypass observability on a critical path; every external call is traced.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. MONOREPO STRUCTURE (Turborepo)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 1.1 Canonical Directory Layout

```
/
├── apps/
│   ├── api/               # Fastify HTTP + WebSocket server
│   ├── worker/            # BullMQ job consumers (Node process)
│   └── mobile/            # React Native (Expo) Android client
├── packages/
│   ├── ai-core/           # Claude agent kernel: runAgent, toolRouter, promptBuilder
│   ├── voice-pipeline/    # Twilio stream handler + ElevenLabs TTS + STT
│   ├── db/                # Prisma client, migrations, repositories, seed
│   ├── queues/            # BullMQ queue/job definitions & producers
│   ├── events/            # Domain event schemas, emitter, and bus
│   ├── redis/             # Redis client factory, session helpers, rate limiting
│   ├── config/            # Env schema (Zod), typed config singleton
│   ├── logger/            # Pino-based structured logger factory
│   ├── observability/     # OpenTelemetry, metrics, tracing, health checks
│   ├── zod-schemas/       # All shared Zod schemas & inferred types
│   └── tsconfig/          # Shared TypeScript configurations
└── turbo.json
```

## 1.2 Package Naming Convention

All packages use the `@xtanbot/` namespace. This is non-negotiable.

```
@xtanbot/ai-core
@xtanbot/voice-pipeline
@xtanbot/db
@xtanbot/queues
@xtanbot/events
@xtanbot/redis
@xtanbot/config
@xtanbot/logger
@xtanbot/observability
@xtanbot/zod-schemas
@xtanbot/tsconfig
```

The generic `@repo/` namespace is retired. All imports must use `@xtanbot/`.

## 1.3 Rules

- `apps/` contains only runnable processes. No business logic lives here.
- `packages/` contains all reusable logic. A package must never import from `apps/`.
- An app may import from packages. Packages may import from other packages,
  but circular dependencies are unconditionally forbidden.
- Every package and app must have its own `package.json` with a `name`
  prefixed `@xtanbot/`.
- Turborepo pipeline tasks: `build`, `lint`, `typecheck`, `test`.
  Each package must declare all tasks it participates in.
- New packages must be proposed via an Architecture Decision Record (ADR)
  before being created. No ad-hoc packages.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. CONFIGURATION & ENVIRONMENT (@xtanbot/config)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 2.1 Pattern

```typescript
// packages/config/src/index.ts
import { z } from "zod";

const EnvSchema = z.object({
  NODE_ENV:              z.enum(["development", "test", "production"]),
  API_PORT:              z.coerce.number().default(3000),
  API_HOST:              z.string().default("0.0.0.0"),

  // Database
  DATABASE_URL:          z.string().url(),

  // Redis
  REDIS_URL:             z.string().url(),

  // Anthropic (Claude)
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:     z.string().min(1),
  ANTHROPIC_MODEL:       z.string().default("claude-opus-4-5"),

  // ElevenLabs
  ELEVENLABS_API_KEY:    z.string().min(1),
  ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID:   z.string().min(1),
  ELEVENLABS_MODEL_ID:   z.string().default("eleven_turbo_v2_5"),

  // Twilio
  TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID:    z.string().min(1),
  TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN:     z.string().min(1),
  TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER:   z.string().min(1),

  // Deepgram (STT)
  DEEPGRAM_API_KEY:      z.string().min(1),

  // Auth
  JWT_SECRET:            z.string().min(32),
  JWT_EXPIRES_IN:        z.string().default("7d"),

  // Worker
  WORKER_CONCURRENCY:    z.coerce.number().default(10),

  // Rate Limiting
  RATE_LIMIT_CALLS_PER_HOUR:     z.coerce.number().default(20),
  RATE_LIMIT_AI_RPM:             z.coerce.number().default(60),
  VOICE_SESSION_MAX_DURATION_S:  z.coerce.number().default(1800),
});

export type Env = z.infer<typeof EnvSchema>;
export const config: Env = EnvSchema.parse(process.env);
```

## 2.2 Rules

- `config` is parsed and validated at process startup. A missing or invalid
  var must throw immediately and crash the process. Silent defaults for
  security-critical values are forbidden.
- Import `config` from `@xtanbot/config` everywhere. Never read `process.env`
  directly outside this package.
- The Zod EnvSchema is the single source of truth for all required variables.
- `.env.example` must be kept in sync with EnvSchema at all times.
  CI must fail if a variable exists in EnvSchema but not in `.env.example`.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. DATABASE LAYER (@xtanbot/db)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 3.1 Pattern

```typescript
// packages/db/src/client.ts
import { PrismaClient } from "@prisma/client";
import { config } from "@xtanbot/config";

const globalForPrisma = globalThis as unknown as { prisma: PrismaClient };

export const prisma =
  globalForPrisma.prisma ??
  new PrismaClient({
    datasources: { db: { url: config.DATABASE_URL } },
    log: config.NODE_ENV === "development" ? ["query", "error"] : ["error"],
  });

if (config.NODE_ENV !== "production") globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;
```

## 3.2 Rules

- There is exactly ONE PrismaClient instance across the entire runtime.
  It lives in `@xtanbot/db`. Nothing else instantiates PrismaClient.
- All Prisma schema changes go through migrations (`prisma migrate dev`).
  Never use `db push` in production. Migrations are version-controlled.
- Repository functions are the only code that calls `prisma.*`. They live in
  `packages/db/src/repositories/`. Each domain gets its own repository file.
- Repository functions must be pure async functions — no side effects beyond
  the database operation.
- Exported types from `@xtanbot/db` are always inferred from Prisma's generated
  types; never manually duplicate model interfaces.
- Soft deletes are preferred over hard deletes for all user-owned entities.
  Add `deletedAt DateTime?` to relevant models.

## 3.3 Repository Naming Convention

```
packages/db/src/repositories/
├── conversation.repository.ts
├── user.repository.ts
├── call.repository.ts
├── meeting.repository.ts
├── contact.repository.ts
├── session.repository.ts
└── index.ts   ← re-exports all repositories
```

Each repository file exports a single frozen object:

```typescript
export const callRepository = {
  async create(data: CreateCall): Promise<Call> { ... },
  async findById(id: string): Promise<Call | null> { ... },
  async update(id: string, data: UpdateCall): Promise<Call> { ... },
  async softDelete(id: string): Promise<void> { ... },
} as const;
```


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. LOGGING (@xtanbot/logger)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 4.1 Pattern

```typescript
// packages/logger/src/index.ts
import pino from "pino";
import { config } from "@xtanbot/config";

export function createLogger(context: string) {
  return pino({
    level: config.NODE_ENV === "production" ? "info" : "debug",
    base: { context, service: "xtanbot" },
    timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime,
    redact: {
      paths: ["*.password", "*.token", "*.apiKey", "*.authorization"],
      censor: "[REDACTED]",
    },
  });
}

export type Logger = ReturnType<typeof createLogger>;
```

## 4.2 Rules

- Every module that emits logs must call `createLogger("ModuleName")` once
  at module scope and reuse the instance.
- Log entries must always include: `context`, `level`, `timestamp`, and a
  `msg`. Attach structured metadata as additional fields — never interpolate
  variable data into the message string.
- Sensitive fields (password, token, apiKey, authorization headers) must
  always be redacted. The logger's redact configuration is the safety net,
  but developers must also never deliberately log these fields.
- Use log levels correctly:
  - `trace` — internal flow tracing (dev only, never in production)
  - `debug` — diagnostic data, intermediate values
  - `info`  — normal operational events (call started, job completed)
  - `warn`  — recoverable anomalies (retry attempt, rate limit approached)
  - `error` — failures requiring attention (always include `err` field)
  - `fatal` — process-killing errors (always followed by process exit)
- Never use `console.log` / `console.error` in application code.
- In production, logs are emitted as newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON) and
  shipped to a centralized logging backend.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. ZOD VALIDATION (@xtanbot/zod-schemas)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 5.1 Rules

- All schemas shared between packages or apps live in `@xtanbot/zod-schemas`.
- App-local schemas (e.g., a single route's request body) may live co-located,
  but must extend or compose from `@xtanbot/zod-schemas` types where overlap exists.
- Every external input boundary must parse with Zod before any other logic:
  - HTTP request bodies, query params, path params
  - WebSocket messages
  - BullMQ job payloads (defined with `z.object` in queue definitions)
  - Claude tool call arguments
  - Domain event payloads
  - ElevenLabs/Twilio webhook payloads
  - Redis session data (on read)
- Use `schema.parse()` when a failure should throw (trusted server-side path).
  Use `schema.safeParse()` when returning a structured user-facing error.
- Always export both the schema and its inferred type:

```typescript
export const CreateCallSchema = z.object({
  userId:      z.string().uuid(),
  toNumber:    z.string().regex(/^\+[1-9]\d{1,14}$/, "E.164 format required"),
  agentPrompt: z.string().max(500).optional(),
});
export type CreateCall = z.infer<typeof CreateCallSchema>;
```

- Schemas must be organized by domain:

```
packages/zod-schemas/src/
├── call.schemas.ts
├── meeting.schemas.ts
├── conversation.schemas.ts
├── contact.schemas.ts
├── session.schemas.ts
├── user.schemas.ts
└── index.ts
```


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. DOMAIN ARCHITECTURE
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 6.1 Philosophy

The system is organized around business domains, not technical layers alone.
Every feature maps to a domain. Every domain owns its schemas, repositories,
services, events, and queue jobs. Infrastructure packages serve all domains
but own none.

## 6.2 Defined Domains

```
conversation   — dialogue history, message threading, context windows
calls          — Twilio phone call lifecycle, call records, status tracking
meetings       — scheduling, calendar integration, invites, reminders
contacts       — external persons the agent interacts with or on behalf of
sessions       — voice call session state, Redis-backed, TTL-enforced
users          — account identity, authentication, preferences
```

## 6.3 Domain Mapping Rules

Each domain follows this mapping:

| Layer              | Location                                                  |
|--------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Schema             | `@xtanbot/zod-schemas/src/{domain}.schemas.ts`            |
| Prisma Model       | `@xtanbot/db/prisma/schema.prisma`                        |
| Repository         | `@xtanbot/db/src/repositories/{domain}.repository.ts`     |
| Service            | `apps/api/src/services/{domain}.service.ts`               |
| Route Handler      | `apps/api/src/routes/v1/{domain}/`                        |
| Domain Events      | `@xtanbot/events/src/events/{domain}.events.ts`           |
| Queue Jobs         | `@xtanbot/queues/src/jobs/{domain}.job.ts`                |
| Worker Handler     | `apps/worker/src/handlers/{domain}.handler.ts`            |

## 6.4 Domain Isolation Rules

- A service for domain A must not directly import a repository for domain B.
  Cross-domain data needs are resolved through domain events or dedicated
  read-model queries in the requesting domain's own repository.
- Domain events are the runtime contract between domains.
  Shared Zod schemas are the compile-time contract between domains.
- Each domain's service is the single authority over mutations to that domain's
  data. No other service may write to another domain's tables.

## 6.5 Example: Calls Domain Structure

```
@xtanbot/zod-schemas      → CreateCallSchema, CallStatusSchema
@xtanbot/db               → callRepository (create, findById, updateStatus)
apps/api/services         → callService (initiateCall, endCall, getCallHistory)
apps/api/routes/v1/calls  → POST /calls, GET /calls/:id, DELETE /calls/:id
@xtanbot/events           → call.started, call.completed, call.failed
@xtanbot/queues           → AgentJobSchema, enqueueAgentJob
apps/worker/handlers      → agentHandler (processes agent queue jobs for calls)
```


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. EVENT ARCHITECTURE (@xtanbot/events)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 7.1 Philosophy

The system is event-driven at the domain boundary. When a significant state
change occurs in one domain, a typed domain event is emitted. Other domains
react by consuming events — they never poll or call across domain service
boundaries synchronously.

## 7.2 Package Structure

```
packages/events/src/
├── events/
│   ├── call.events.ts         # call.started, call.completed, call.failed
│   ├── meeting.events.ts      # meeting.scheduled, meeting.cancelled
│   ├── conversation.events.ts # agent.responded, context.updated
│   ├── session.events.ts      # session.created, session.expired
│   └── index.ts
├── bus.ts                     # Event bus (Redis pub/sub backed)
├── emitter.ts                 # Type-safe emit helper
└── types.ts                   # DomainEvent base type
```

## 7.3 Domain Event Schema Pattern

```typescript
// packages/events/src/events/call.events.ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const CallStartedEventSchema = z.object({
  type:      z.literal("call.started"),
  callId:    z.string().uuid(),
  userId:    z.string().uuid(),
  callSid:   z.string(),
  toNumber:  z.string(),
  timestamp: z.string().datetime(),
});
export type CallStartedEvent = z.infer<typeof CallStartedEventSchema>;

export const CallCompletedEventSchema = z.object({
  type:       z.literal("call.completed"),
  callId:     z.string().uuid(),
  userId:     z.string().uuid(),
  durationMs: z.number().int().nonnegative(),
  timestamp:  z.string().datetime(),
});
export type CallCompletedEvent = z.infer<typeof CallCompletedEventSchema>;

export const CallEventSchema = z.discriminatedUnion("type", [
  CallStartedEventSchema,
  CallCompletedEventSchema,
]);
export type CallEvent = z.infer<typeof CallEventSchema>;
```

## 7.4 Canonical Domain Events

```
call.started              — Twilio call connected, session created
call.completed            — Call ended normally
call.failed               — Call dropped or errored
meeting.scheduled         — Meeting created and confirmed
meeting.cancelled         — Meeting removed
meeting.reminder.sent     — Reminder notification dispatched
agent.responded           — Claude completed a turn, response ready
agent.tool_called         — A tool was invoked during agent reasoning
session.created           — Voice session initialized in Redis
session.expired           — TTL elapsed, session purged
contact.created           — New contact added to the system
conversation.updated      — Conversation history appended
```

## 7.5 Event Bus Pattern

```typescript
// packages/events/src/bus.ts
import { redisConnection } from "@xtanbot/redis";
import { createLogger } from "@xtanbot/logger";
import type { DomainEvent } from "./types";

const logger = createLogger("EventBus");

export async function publishEvent(event: DomainEvent): Promise<void> {
  const channel = `xtanbot:events:${event.type}`;
  await redisConnection.publish(channel, JSON.stringify(event));
  logger.info({ eventType: event.type }, "Domain event published");
}

export function subscribeToEvent(
  eventType: string,
  handler: (event: DomainEvent) => Promise<void>
): void {
  const subscriber = redisConnection.duplicate();
  subscriber.subscribe(`xtanbot:events:${eventType}`);
  subscriber.on("message", async (_, message) => {
    try {
      await handler(JSON.parse(message) as DomainEvent);
    } catch (err) {
      logger.error({ err, eventType }, "Event handler failed");
    }
  });
}
```

## 7.6 Rules

- Every domain event must have a Zod schema. Unschematized events are forbidden.
- Events are immutable facts. Once emitted, they cannot be retracted.
  Compensate with a new event (e.g., `call.cancelled` after `call.started`).
- Services emit events after a successful state change — never before.
- Event handlers must be idempotent. The bus may deliver an event more than once.
- Events carry only identifiers and essential scalars — not full entity graphs.
  Consumers fetch entity data from repositories when needed.
- Event type names follow `{domain}.{action}` dot notation.
- All event channel names follow: `xtanbot:events:{event.type}`.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8. AI AGENT KERNEL (@xtanbot/ai-core)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 8.1 Architecture Overview

The AI system is governed by a central kernel. Every reasoning operation —
without exception — flows through this kernel. No application code, service,
or worker calls the Claude API directly.

```
@xtanbot/ai-core
├── src/
│   ├── agent.ts            # Kernel entry point: runAgent()
│   ├── tool-router.ts      # Central tool registry + dispatcher
│   ├── prompt-builder.ts   # Composes system prompts from versioned templates
│   ├── context-builder.ts  # Assembles AgentContext from session + history
│   ├── client.ts           # Single Anthropic SDK instance (private, not exported)
│   ├── tools/
│   │   ├── schedule-meeting.tool.ts
│   │   ├── initiate-call.tool.ts
│   │   ├── lookup-contact.tool.ts
│   │   ├── get-weather.tool.ts
│   │   ├── search-calendar.tool.ts
│   │   └── index.ts
│   ├── prompts/            # See Section 9: Prompt Governance
│   ├── errors.ts           # AgentError, ToolError, ContextError
│   └── types.ts            # AgentContext, AgentResponse, ToolDefinition, LLMProvider
```

## 8.2 The Agent Kernel — Four Components

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│              AGENT KERNEL                   │
│                                             │
│  ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐   │
│  │  runAgent() │───▶│  promptBuilder   │   │
│  │             │    └──────────────────┘   │
│  │  agentic    │    ┌──────────────────┐   │
│  │  loop       │───▶│  agentContext    │   │
│  │             │    └──────────────────┘   │
│  │             │    ┌──────────────────┐   │
│  │             │───▶│  toolRouter      │   │
│  └─────────────┘    └──────────────────┘   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

- `runAgent(ctx)` — Orchestrates the agentic loop. Stateless. All context
  arrives via `AgentContext`. Returns `AgentResponse`.
- `toolRouter` — Singleton registry. Dispatches all tool calls. Validates
  inputs with each tool's own Zod schema.
- `promptBuilder` — Composes the system prompt from versioned templates.
  Never concatenates raw strings.
- `agentContext` — Serializable, immutable context. Carries: messages,
  session metadata, user preferences, call state.

## 8.3 Tool Router

```typescript
// packages/ai-core/src/tool-router.ts
import { allTools } from "./tools";
import { ToolError } from "./errors";
import { createLogger } from "@xtanbot/logger";
import type { ToolDefinition } from "./types";

const logger = createLogger("ToolRouter");

class ToolRouter {
  private readonly registry = new Map<string, ToolDefinition>();

  constructor(tools: ToolDefinition[]) {
    for (const tool of tools) this.registry.set(tool.name, tool);
    logger.info({ toolCount: this.registry.size }, "Tool router initialized");
  }

  async dispatch(name: string, args: unknown): Promise<unknown> {
    const tool = this.registry.get(name);
    if (!tool) throw new ToolError(`Unknown tool: ${name}`);
    const validated = tool.inputSchema.parse(args);
    logger.debug({ toolName: name }, "Tool dispatched");
    return tool.execute(validated);
  }

  getDefinitions() {
    return [...this.registry.values()].map((t) => t.toClaudeToolDefinition());
  }
}

export const toolRouter = new ToolRouter(allTools);
```

## 8.4 Tool Definition Contract

```typescript
// packages/ai-core/src/types.ts
import { z } from "zod";
import type { Tool } from "@anthropic-ai/sdk/resources";

export interface ToolDefinition<TInput = unknown, TOutput = unknown> {
  name:        string;
  description: string;
  inputSchema: z.ZodSchema<TInput>;
  execute:     (input: TInput) => Promise<TOutput>;
  toClaudeToolDefinition(): Tool;
}

export type AgentContext = {
  readonly sessionId:     string;
  readonly userId:        string;
  readonly callSid?:      string;
  readonly messages:      ReadonlyArray<AgentMessage>;
  readonly userProfile?:  UserProfile;
  readonly callMetadata?: CallMetadata;
};

export type AgentResponse = {
  readonly text:       string;
  readonly toolsUsed:  ReadonlyArray<string>;
  readonly stopReason: "end_turn" | "max_tokens";
  readonly usage:      { inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number };
};
```

## 8.5 Agentic Loop

```typescript
// packages/ai-core/src/agent.ts
import { anthropicClient } from "./client";
import { toolRouter }      from "./tool-router";
import { buildSystemPrompt } from "./prompt-builder";
import { AgentError }      from "./errors";
import { createLogger }    from "@xtanbot/logger";
import { config }          from "@xtanbot/config";
import type { AgentContext, AgentResponse } from "./types";

const logger = createLogger("AgentKernel");

export async function runAgent(ctx: AgentContext): Promise<AgentResponse> {
  logger.info({ sessionId: ctx.sessionId, userId: ctx.userId }, "Agent loop started");

  const messages = [...ctx.messages];
  const toolsUsed: string[] = [];

  while (true) {
    const response = await anthropicClient.messages.create({
      model:      config.ANTHROPIC_MODEL,
      system:     buildSystemPrompt(ctx),
      tools:      toolRouter.getDefinitions(),
      messages,
      max_tokens: 1024,
    });

    if (response.stop_reason === "end_turn") {
      logger.info({ sessionId: ctx.sessionId, toolsUsed }, "Agent loop completed");
      return extractFinalResponse(response, toolsUsed);
    }

    if (response.stop_reason === "tool_use") {
      const toolResults = await Promise.all(
        response.content
          .filter((b) => b.type === "tool_use")
          .map(async (b) => {
            if (b.type !== "tool_use") return null;
            toolsUsed.push(b.name);
            const result = await toolRouter.dispatch(b.name, b.input);
            return { type: "tool_result" as const, tool_use_id: b.id, content: JSON.stringify(result) };
          })
      );
      messages.push({ role: "assistant", content: response.content });
      messages.push({ role: "user", content: toolResults.filter(Boolean) });
      continue;
    }

    throw new AgentError(`Unexpected stop_reason: ${response.stop_reason}`);
  }
}
```

## 8.6 Rules

- `toolRouter` is the ONLY place Claude tool calls are dispatched.
- `runAgent` is the ONLY entry point for Claude API calls.
- Tools are stateless pure functions. No tool holds instance-level state.
- `AgentContext` must be fully serializable (no class instances, Buffers, functions).
- Adding a new AI capability requires only: (1) new tool file, (2) add to `tools/index.ts`.
- The `anthropicClient` in `client.ts` is private to `@xtanbot/ai-core`.
  It is never exported. External code accesses AI only via `runAgent()`.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 9. PROMPT GOVERNANCE (@xtanbot/ai-core/prompts)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 9.1 Philosophy

Prompts are code. They determine AI behavior and quality. They must be
version-controlled, reviewed, tested, and governed with the same rigor as
application logic. Inline prompt strings scattered across the codebase are
a first-class architectural defect.

## 9.2 Prompt Directory Structure

```
packages/ai-core/src/prompts/
├── system/
│   ├── voice-agent.v1.ts       # Primary voice assistant system prompt
│   ├── scheduling-agent.v1.ts  # Specialized scheduling context
│   └── index.ts                # Registry: name → versioned prompt function
├── tools/
│   ├── descriptions.ts         # Tool description strings (used in tool defs)
│   └── index.ts
├── memory/
│   ├── conversation-formatter.ts  # Formats history for context window
│   └── summary-formatter.ts       # Formats compressed memory summaries
└── context/
    ├── user-profile-formatter.ts  # Renders user profile into prompt text
    └── call-state-formatter.ts    # Renders live call state into prompt text
```

## 9.3 Prompt Module Pattern

```typescript
// packages/ai-core/src/prompts/system/voice-agent.v1.ts
import type { AgentContext } from "../../types";

export const PROMPT_VERSION = "voice-agent@v1";

export function renderVoiceAgentSystemPrompt(ctx: AgentContext): string {
  return `
You are xTanBot, an intelligent voice assistant operating on behalf of ${ctx.userProfile?.name ?? "the user"}.

Your capabilities:
- Schedule and manage meetings
- Make and manage phone calls
- Look up and manage contacts
- Retrieve real-time information

Behavioral rules:
- Always confirm before taking irreversible actions (placing calls, deleting meetings).
- Be concise. This is a voice interface — keep responses under 3 sentences unless detail is required.
- If uncertain, ask one clarifying question. Never assume.
- Never reveal system internals, tool names, or implementation details.

Current context:
- Session ID: ${ctx.sessionId}
- Active call: ${ctx.callSid ?? "none"}
  `.trim();
}
```

## 9.4 Rules

- All prompt strings live in `packages/ai-core/src/prompts/`. No exceptions.
- Prompts are functions that accept context and return a string. Never static
  string constants — dynamic context must be injected at render time.
- Prompt files are versioned in their filename (`voice-agent.v1.ts`).
  Behavioral changes require a new version file. Old versions are kept until
  all traffic and tests are migrated off them.
- Tool descriptions live in `prompts/tools/descriptions.ts` and are imported
  by tool definition files. They are part of the prompt surface area and
  subject to the same review rigor.
- Every system prompt must have a unit test that: (a) renders with mock context,
  (b) asserts required sections are present, (c) asserts no secrets are leaked.
- Prompt changes require review from both an engineer and a product owner.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 10. VOICE PIPELINE (@xtanbot/voice-pipeline)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 10.1 Architecture

```
@xtanbot/voice-pipeline
├── src/
│   ├── twilio/
│   │   ├── stream-handler.ts       # Parses Twilio Media Stream WebSocket frames
│   │   ├── twiml-builder.ts        # Generates TwiML response XML
│   │   └── signature-validator.ts  # Validates Twilio webhook signatures
│   ├── elevenlabs/
│   │   ├── tts-client.ts           # ElevenLabs streaming TTS (chunk forwarding)
│   │   └── voice-config.ts         # Voice ID, model, stability settings
│   ├── transcription/
│   │   └── stt-client.ts           # STT abstraction (Deepgram primary)
│   ├── pipeline.ts                 # Orchestrates the full audio flow
│   ├── errors.ts                   # PipelineError, STTError, TTSError
│   └── types.ts                    # AudioFrame, TranscriptResult, PipelineSession
```

## 10.2 Pipeline Flow (Enforced — Unidirectional)

```
Twilio WebSocket (μ-law audio frames)
  → stream-handler.ts         [decode, buffer, detect end-of-utterance]
    → stt-client.ts           [stream to Deepgram, receive final transcript]
      → @xtanbot/queues       [enqueueAgentJob with transcript + sessionId]
        → [BullMQ picks up job]
          → apps/worker       [agent.handler.ts]
            → runAgent()      [@xtanbot/ai-core]
              → toolRouter    [if tools invoked]
            → tts-client.ts   [stream ElevenLabs audio chunks]
              → Twilio WS     [forward audio chunks as Base64 media frames]
```

No step in this chain may reach backward or skip a step.

## 10.3 Rules

- Audio processing (buffering, encoding, end-of-utterance detection) is
  isolated to `twilio/stream-handler.ts`. No other file handles raw audio bytes.
- TTS streaming must use ElevenLabs' streaming endpoint. Audio chunks must
  be forwarded to Twilio as they arrive. Full-buffer-then-send is forbidden.
  Voice latency is a product-critical metric.
- The pipeline is stateless. Session state lives in Redis, keyed by
  `voice:session:{sessionId}`. The pipeline reads and writes; it does not own state.
- Twilio webhook signature validation is mandatory on every inbound request
  and WebSocket upgrade. Requests failing validation are rejected HTTP 403.
- Audio format: Twilio sends μ-law (PCMU) 8kHz. Deepgram accepts this natively.
  ElevenLabs requires PCM 16-bit. Conversion happens inside the pipeline.
- End-of-utterance detection uses Deepgram's endpointing. When a transcript is
  marked `is_final: true`, the pipeline enqueues the agent job.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 11. QUEUE SYSTEM (@xtanbot/queues)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 11.1 Structure

```
packages/queues/src/
├── queues/
│   ├── agent.queue.ts         # AI agent reasoning queue
│   ├── tts.queue.ts           # TTS pre-rendering queue
│   └── notification.queue.ts  # User notifications
├── jobs/
│   ├── agent.job.ts           # AgentJobSchema + enqueueAgentJob()
│   ├── tts.job.ts             # TtsJobSchema + enqueueTtsJob()
│   └── notification.job.ts
└── index.ts
```

## 11.2 Job Definition Pattern

```typescript
// packages/queues/src/jobs/agent.job.ts
import { Queue } from "bullmq";
import { z } from "zod";
import { redisConnection } from "@xtanbot/redis";

export const AgentJobSchema = z.object({
  sessionId:  z.string().uuid(),
  userId:     z.string().uuid(),
  transcript: z.string().min(1).max(2000),
  callSid:    z.string().min(1),
});
export type AgentJob = z.infer<typeof AgentJobSchema>;

export const AGENT_QUEUE_NAME = "xtanbot:agent" as const;
export const AGENT_JOB_NAME  = "agent:process" as const;

export const agentQueue = new Queue<AgentJob>(AGENT_QUEUE_NAME, {
  connection: redisConnection,
  defaultJobOptions: {
    attempts: 3,
    backoff: { type: "exponential", delay: 1000 },
    removeOnComplete: 100,
    removeOnFail: 500,
  },
});

export async function enqueueAgentJob(data: AgentJob): Promise<void> {
  AgentJobSchema.parse(data);
  await agentQueue.add(AGENT_JOB_NAME, data);
}
```

## 11.3 Rules

- Queue definitions (Queue instances) live in `@xtanbot/queues` only.
- Queue names: `xtanbot:{domain}`. Job names: `{domain}:{action}`.
  No inline magic strings at call sites.
- Job payloads are validated with Zod before enqueue AND in the worker handler.
- All queues share the single Redis connection from `@xtanbot/redis`.
- Failed jobs that exhaust retries emit a domain event so the system responds
  gracefully (e.g., informing the caller the assistant is unavailable).


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 12. REDIS (@xtanbot/redis)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 12.1 Pattern

```typescript
// packages/redis/src/client.ts
import IORedis from "ioredis";
import { config } from "@xtanbot/config";

export const redisConnection = new IORedis(config.REDIS_URL, {
  maxRetriesPerRequest: null,
  enableReadyCheck: false,
  lazyConnect: false,
});
```

## 12.2 Key Naming Convention

```
voice:session:{sessionId}         — Active voice call session
cache:contact:{userId}:{query}    — Contact lookup cache
rate:calls:{userId}               — Per-user call rate limit counter
rate:ai:{userId}                  — Per-user AI rate limit counter
lock:call:{callSid}               — Distributed lock for call operations
xtanbot:events:{event.type}       — Pub/sub event channels
```

## 12.3 Rules

- One `IORedis` instance (`redisConnection`) shared across BullMQ, pub/sub,
  session, and cache operations. Never create a second connection.
- All TTLs are mandatory. Every key written to Redis must have an expiry.
  Perpetual keys are a memory leak and an operational incident waiting to happen.
- Session data read from Redis must be validated with Zod before use.
- Redis is for: session state, rate limiting, pub/sub, distributed locks,
  job queues, and short-lived caches. Not a primary data store.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 13. RATE LIMITING AND SAFETY
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 13.1 Philosophy

Rate limiting and safety controls are not optional features — they are
architectural requirements. The system operates on behalf of users and
interacts with external telephony infrastructure. Abuse or misconfiguration
can cause real-world harm and significant cost.

## 13.2 Redis-Backed Rate Limiter Pattern

```typescript
// packages/redis/src/rate-limiter.ts
import { redisConnection } from "./client";

export type RateLimitResult =
  | { allowed: true;  remaining: number }
  | { allowed: false; retryAfterSeconds: number };

export async function checkRateLimit(
  key: string,
  limit: number,
  windowSeconds: number
): Promise<RateLimitResult> {
  const count = await redisConnection.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redisConnection.expire(key, windowSeconds);
  if (count > limit) {
    const ttl = await redisConnection.ttl(key);
    return { allowed: false, retryAfterSeconds: ttl };
  }
  return { allowed: true, remaining: limit - count };
}
```

## 13.3 Enforced Rate Limits

| Limit                    | Key Pattern              | Default      | Config Key                       |
|--------------------------|--------------------------|--------------|----------------------------------|
| Calls per user per hour  | `rate:calls:{userId}`    | 20 / hour    | `RATE_LIMIT_CALLS_PER_HOUR`      |
| AI requests per minute   | `rate:ai:{userId}`       | 60 / min     | `RATE_LIMIT_AI_RPM`              |
| Voice session max length | enforced in pipeline.ts  | 30 minutes   | `VOICE_SESSION_MAX_DURATION_S`   |
| Webhook requests per IP  | `rate:webhook:{ip}`      | 100 / min    | (hardcoded, not user-configurable)|

All rate limit thresholds must be configurable via `@xtanbot/config`.

## 13.4 Twilio Webhook Security

- Every inbound Twilio request and WebSocket upgrade must have its
  `X-Twilio-Signature` validated before any processing.
- Validation uses `twilio.validateRequest()` in `signature-validator.ts`.
- Replay attack prevention: each webhook's `CallSid` is stored in Redis with
  a 5-minute TTL. Duplicate CallSids within the window are rejected HTTP 409.
- Requests failing validation are rejected HTTP 403, logged at `warn` level.
- `TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN` is never logged, never in error responses, never in Redis.

## 13.5 General Safety Rules

- All user-facing inputs are Zod-validated before reaching any service.
- Phone numbers are normalized to E.164 at the input boundary.
- The agent must always verbally confirm irreversible actions before executing
  them (enforced in system prompt and audited in integration tests).
- No PII (names, phone numbers, transcripts) must appear in log output.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 14. OBSERVABILITY (@xtanbot/observability)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 14.1 Philosophy

A production AI voice system is complex, real-time, and multi-component.
Observability is not bolted on after the fact — it is a first-class
architectural concern. Every critical path must be traceable, every
performance regression detectable, every failure diagnosable without
local reproduction.

## 14.2 Package Structure

```
packages/observability/src/
├── tracer.ts     # OpenTelemetry TracerProvider setup and initTracing()
├── metrics.ts    # Prometheus metrics registry + all named metric definitions
├── health.ts     # Health check registry + Fastify plugin
├── middleware.ts # Fastify request tracing middleware
└── index.ts
```

## 14.3 Required Named Metrics

```typescript
// HTTP
http_request_duration_ms      — Histogram, labels: method, route, status_code
http_requests_total           — Counter,   labels: method, route, status_code

// Voice Pipeline
voice_call_duration_ms        — Histogram, labels: status (completed|failed)
voice_stt_latency_ms          — Histogram, labels: provider
voice_tts_latency_ms          — Histogram, labels: provider, stream_first_byte
voice_active_sessions         — Gauge

// AI Agent
agent_loop_duration_ms        — Histogram, labels: tools_used_count
agent_tool_calls_total        — Counter,   labels: tool_name, success
agent_token_usage_total       — Counter,   labels: direction (input|output)

// Queue System
queue_job_processing_ms       — Histogram, labels: queue_name
queue_jobs_total              — Counter,   labels: queue_name, status
queue_depth                   — Gauge,     labels: queue_name

// Business
calls_initiated_total         — Counter,   labels: outcome
meetings_scheduled_total      — Counter
rate_limit_hits_total         — Counter,   labels: limit_type
```

## 14.4 Health Checks

Fastify exposes `GET /health` (liveness) and `GET /health/ready` (readiness).
Readiness fails if Postgres or Redis are unreachable.

Registered checks: postgres (SELECT 1), redis (PING), elevenlabs (reachability),
deepgram (reachability), bullmq (queue client connected).

## 14.5 Rules

- `initTracing(serviceName)` must be the very first statement in every app's
  entry point, before any other imports.
- Every external service call (Anthropic, ElevenLabs, Deepgram, Twilio, Prisma)
  must be wrapped in an OpenTelemetry span.
- All named metrics are defined and exported from `@xtanbot/observability/src/metrics.ts`.
  Metrics defined elsewhere are forbidden.
- Fastify request tracing is applied globally. No route is exempt.
- Traces, logs, and metrics share a `traceId` within a request context.
  The `traceId` must be included in log records for traced requests.
- Health check endpoints are exempt from auth middleware and rate limiting.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 15. FASTIFY API APP (apps/api)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 15.1 Structure

```
apps/api/src/
├── index.ts               # Process entry: initTracing(), then startServer()
├── server.ts              # Fastify instance creation, plugin registration
├── plugins/
│   ├── auth.plugin.ts
│   ├── cors.plugin.ts
│   ├── rate-limit.plugin.ts
│   └── websocket.plugin.ts
├── routes/
│   ├── v1/
│   │   ├── calls/
│   │   ├── meetings/
│   │   ├── contacts/
│   │   └── users/
│   └── twilio/
│       ├── webhook.handler.ts
│       └── stream.handler.ts
├── services/
│   ├── call.service.ts
│   ├── meeting.service.ts
│   ├── contact.service.ts
│   └── user.service.ts
└── middleware/
    └── validate-twilio-signature.ts
```

## 15.2 Route Handler Pattern

```typescript
// Handlers are thin: validate → delegate → respond. Nothing more.
export async function handleCreateCall(
  req: FastifyRequest<{ Body: CreateCall }>,
  reply: FastifyReply
): Promise<void> {
  const body = CreateCallSchema.parse(req.body);
  const result = await callService.initiateCall({ ...body, userId: req.user.id });
  return reply.code(201).send(result);
}
```

## 15.3 Rules

- Route handlers contain NO business logic.
- Business logic lives in `apps/api/src/services/`.
- WebSocket routes for Twilio streams are in a dedicated plugin.
  Never mix HTTP and WebSocket logic.
- Every route must declare a Fastify JSON Schema for request and response.
- HTTP error responses: `{ error: string; message: string; code?: string }`.
- Plugins that decorate the Fastify instance must use `fastify-plugin` (fp).
- `index.ts` calls `initTracing("xtanbot-api")` before any other imports.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 16. SERVICE LAYER
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 16.1 Pattern

```typescript
// apps/api/src/services/call.service.ts
import { enqueueAgentJob } from "@xtanbot/queues";
import { callRepository }  from "@xtanbot/db";
import { publishEvent }    from "@xtanbot/events";
import { checkRateLimit }  from "@xtanbot/redis";
import { createLogger }    from "@xtanbot/logger";
import { config }          from "@xtanbot/config";
import type { CreateCall } from "@xtanbot/zod-schemas";

const logger = createLogger("CallService");

export const callService = {
  async initiateCall(data: CreateCall & { userId: string }): Promise<Call> {
    logger.info({ userId: data.userId }, "Initiating call");

    const rateCheck = await checkRateLimit(
      `rate:calls:${data.userId}`,
      config.RATE_LIMIT_CALLS_PER_HOUR,
      3600
    );
    if (!rateCheck.allowed) {
      throw new RateLimitError(`Retry in ${rateCheck.retryAfterSeconds}s`);
    }

    const call = await callRepository.create(data);

    await publishEvent({
      type: "call.started", callId: call.id, userId: data.userId,
      callSid: call.callSid, toNumber: data.toNumber,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    });

    logger.info({ callId: call.id }, "Call created, event published");
    return call;
  },
} as const;
```

## 16.2 Rules

- Services are stateless. No mutable module-level or instance-level variables.
- Cross-app communication uses queues or events only.
- Services emit domain events after successful state changes.
- Services own transactions. Multi-table atomic operations use `prisma.$transaction()`.
- Services apply rate limiting before any external call or AI request.
- Service functions log at entry (`info`) and on error (`error` with `err` field).


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 17. WORKER APP (apps/worker)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 17.1 Structure

```
apps/worker/src/
├── index.ts               # Entry: initTracing(), register workers, graceful shutdown
├── workers/
│   ├── agent.worker.ts
│   ├── tts.worker.ts
│   └── notification.worker.ts
└── handlers/
    ├── agent.handler.ts
    └── tts.handler.ts
```

## 17.2 Worker Pattern

```typescript
// apps/worker/src/workers/agent.worker.ts
import { Worker } from "bullmq";
import { redisConnection }                    from "@xtanbot/redis";
import { AgentJobSchema, AGENT_QUEUE_NAME }   from "@xtanbot/queues";
import { createLogger }                       from "@xtanbot/logger";
import { config }                             from "@xtanbot/config";
import { agentHandler }                       from "../handlers/agent.handler";

const logger = createLogger("AgentWorker");

export const agentWorker = new Worker(
  AGENT_QUEUE_NAME,
  async (job) => {
    const data = AgentJobSchema.parse(job.data);
    logger.info({ jobId: job.id, sessionId: data.sessionId }, "Agent job started");
    return agentHandler.process(data);
  },
  { connection: redisConnection, concurrency: config.WORKER_CONCURRENCY }
);

agentWorker.on("failed", (job, err) => {
  logger.error(
    { jobId: job?.id, queue: AGENT_QUEUE_NAME, attempt: job?.attemptsMade, err },
    "Agent job failed"
  );
});
```

## 17.3 Rules

- Workers registered in `index.ts` with SIGTERM/SIGINT graceful shutdown.
- `index.ts` calls `initTracing("xtanbot-worker")` as the first statement.
- Worker files only wire a queue to a handler. Logic lives in handlers.
- Concurrency is explicitly set on every Worker. Never leave at default.
- Workers never perform blocking synchronous I/O.
- Job payloads are re-validated with Zod inside each handler (defense in depth).


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 18. REACT NATIVE APP (apps/mobile)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 18.1 Structure

```
apps/mobile/src/
├── app/                  # Expo Router file-based routing
├── components/
│   ├── atoms/
│   ├── molecules/
│   └── organisms/
├── hooks/
│   ├── useVoiceStream.ts # All WebSocket + audio logic — isolated here only
│   ├── useCall.ts
│   └── useMeetings.ts
├── services/
│   ├── call.api.ts
│   ├── meeting.api.ts
│   └── http.ts
├── stores/
│   ├── call.store.ts
│   ├── meeting.store.ts
│   └── user.store.ts
├── utils/
└── constants/
```

## 18.2 Rules

- Import types from `@xtanbot/zod-schemas`. Never redefine shared types.
- All API calls go through `src/services/`. No `fetch` in components.
- API service functions validate responses with Zod schemas.
- State management: Zustand. One store per domain. No nested stores.
- Voice/WebSocket logic is isolated to `hooks/useVoiceStream.ts`.
  Components receive callbacks only — never raw WebSocket handles.
- No business logic in components. Components render and emit events.
- All async hooks explicitly track `loading`, `error`, and `data` states.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 19. TYPESCRIPT CONVENTIONS
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- `strict: true` is mandatory in every tsconfig.
- Base tsconfig is `@xtanbot/tsconfig/base.json`. Every package/app extends it.
- `noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true` must be enabled.
- Prefer `type` over `interface` for data shapes.
  Use `interface` only for shapes that will be implemented or extended.
- Never use `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow with Zod or type guards.
- Never use non-null assertion (`!`) except in test files.
- TypeScript `enum` declarations are forbidden. Use `z.enum([...])` or `as const`.
- All async functions must have explicit return types.
- Barrel files (`index.ts`) are allowed only at package boundaries.
- `readonly` must be used for arrays and object properties that should not mutate.
  Prefer `ReadonlyArray<T>` over `T[]` in all function signatures.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 20. ERROR HANDLING
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- All errors must be typed. Each package defines its domain error classes:

```typescript
export class AgentError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string, public readonly cause?: unknown) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "AgentError";
  }
}
```

- Catch errors at the boundary where they can be meaningfully handled.
- Unhandled promise rejections are a fatal bug. Always handle rejections.
- API error response shape:

```typescript
type ApiError = {
  error:   string;  // machine-readable: "RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED"
  message: string;  // human-readable description
  code?:   string;  // optional sub-code
};
```

- In production, stack traces are never sent to API consumers.
  Internal detail is logged server-side and referenced via request ID.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 21. TESTING
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Test framework: Vitest for all packages and apps.
- Unit tests live adjacent as `*.test.ts` or in `__tests__/`.
- Integration tests requiring Redis or Postgres use docker-compose services
  and live in `tests/integration/` at the package root.
- AI tools must have unit tests that mock the Claude API and verify:
  (a) tool is correctly dispatched by the router,
  (b) Zod validation rejects malformed args,
  (c) execute returns the correct shape.
- Every repository function must have an integration test.
- System prompts must have render tests (see Section 9.4).
- Services must be unit-tested with mocked repositories, queues, and events.
- Voice pipeline latency must have a benchmark asserting STT→agent→TTS
  round-trip stays under 2000ms at p95.
- Coverage thresholds (CI-enforced): statements ≥ 80%, branches ≥ 75%.
- Test files may use `any` and non-null assertions.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 22. FUTURE EXTENSIBILITY
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 22.1 Philosophy

The system is built for a single stack today but must not be imprisoned by it.
Claude, ElevenLabs, Deepgram, and Twilio are current providers. The architecture
must allow any of them to be replaced without rewriting business logic.
Abstractions are the boundary between strategy and implementation.

## 22.2 LLM Provider Abstraction

```typescript
// packages/ai-core/src/types.ts
export interface LLMProvider {
  complete(params: LLMCompletionParams): Promise<LLMCompletionResult>;
  streamComplete(params: LLMCompletionParams): AsyncIterable<LLMStreamChunk>;
}

// Current: ClaudeProvider implements LLMProvider
// Future:  OpenAIProvider, GeminiProvider — drop-in via config flag
```

Switching providers requires only: (1) new implementation of `LLMProvider`,
(2) a config flag to select it. Zero changes to business logic.

## 22.3 Voice Provider Abstraction

```typescript
export interface TTSProvider {
  streamSynthesize(text: string, voiceId: string): AsyncIterable<Buffer>;
}

export interface STTProvider {
  streamTranscribe(audio: AsyncIterable<Buffer>): AsyncIterable<TranscriptResult>;
}

// Current: ElevenLabsProvider (TTS), DeepgramProvider (STT)
// Future:  Any compliant implementation, selected via config
```

## 22.4 Multi-Agent Workflows

The current system runs a single agent per call. Multi-agent support is
structurally enabled by these existing constraints (which must be maintained):

- `AgentContext` is serializable — passable between processes.
- `runAgent()` is a pure function — does not own I/O or network connections.
- Tool results may include `{ delegateTo: agentName, context: AgentContext }`
  as a reserved shape for future supervisor-to-sub-agent handoff.
- Queue-based architecture allows agent tasks to be spawned and consumed
  independently.

## 22.5 Multi-Telephony Provider Support

All Twilio-specific code is isolated in `voice-pipeline/src/twilio/`.
Adding a second provider (Vonage, Bandwidth) requires:
(1) new subdirectory `voice-pipeline/src/{provider}/`,
(2) implementing `AudioStreamHandler` and `WebhookValidator` interfaces,
(3) a config flag to route inbound calls to the correct handler.

## 22.6 Rules

- New provider integrations must implement the defined interface before any
  application code uses them. No direct SDK calls in business logic.
- Provider selection must be configurable via `@xtanbot/config`, not code changes.
- Define abstractions before the second implementation is needed, not after.
- Interfaces live in `types.ts` of the owning package.
  Implementations live in provider-named subdirectories.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 23. CODE GENERATION DIRECTIVES FOR AI ASSISTANTS
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

When generating code for this project, you MUST:

1.  ALWAYS identify which package or app the code belongs to before writing.
2.  ALWAYS use `@xtanbot/` namespace. The `@repo/` namespace is retired.
3.  ALWAYS import from the canonical shared package:
    - Logger      → `import { createLogger } from "@xtanbot/logger"`
    - DB          → `import { prisma } from "@xtanbot/db"`
    - Config      → `import { config } from "@xtanbot/config"`
    - Claude      → `import { runAgent } from "@xtanbot/ai-core"`
    - Queues      → `import { enqueueAgentJob } from "@xtanbot/queues"`
    - Events      → `import { publishEvent } from "@xtanbot/events"`
    - Metrics     → `import { metrics } from "@xtanbot/observability"`
    - Rate limit  → `import { checkRateLimit } from "@xtanbot/redis"`
4.  ALWAYS add a Zod schema before any function that accepts external data.
5.  ALWAYS call `createLogger("ContextName")` at module scope in services/handlers.
6.  ALWAYS emit a domain event after a significant state change in a service.
7.  ALWAYS apply rate limiting before external calls or AI requests in services.
8.  NEVER generate `console.log`. Use `logger.info` / `logger.debug`.
9.  NEVER generate `new PrismaClient()` outside `@xtanbot/db`.
10. NEVER generate `new Anthropic()` outside `@xtanbot/ai-core`.
11. NEVER generate `new Queue()` outside `@xtanbot/queues`.
12. NEVER write a prompt string inline. Place it in `@xtanbot/ai-core/prompts/`.
13. When adding a new AI tool: (a) tool file, (b) Zod schema, (c) add to `tools/index.ts`.
14. When adding a new API endpoint, generate in order:
    (a) Zod schema → `@xtanbot/zod-schemas`,
    (b) repository function → `@xtanbot/db`,
    (c) service function → `apps/api/src/services/`,
    (d) thin route handler,
    (e) route registration,
    (f) domain event definition → `@xtanbot/events`.
15. When uncertain about where logic belongs, default to the most specific
    shared package. If still uncertain, ask before generating.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 24. ANTI-PATTERNS (FORBIDDEN)
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The following are unconditionally forbidden. A PR containing any of these
must not be merged under any circumstances.

| Anti-Pattern                                            | Why Forbidden                                           |
|---------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| `new PrismaClient()` outside `@xtanbot/db`              | Breaks connection pooling, violates single source       |
| `process.env.X` outside `@xtanbot/config`               | Bypasses validation, silent undefined crashes           |
| `new Anthropic()` outside `@xtanbot/ai-core`            | Bypasses tool router, untraceable AI calls              |
| `@repo/` namespace anywhere in the codebase             | Retired — all packages are `@xtanbot/`                  |
| Direct ElevenLabs/Twilio SDK calls in route handlers    | Violates service layer, untestable                      |
| Mutable module-level variables in services              | Breaks statelessness, causes concurrency bugs           |
| `any` type in non-test code                             | Destroys type safety                                    |
| `console.log` / `console.error`                         | Unstructured, unobservable in production                |
| Business logic in route handlers                        | Untestable, violates separation of concerns             |
| Cross-app imports (`apps/api` ↔ `apps/worker`)          | Breaks process isolation                                |
| Hardcoded API keys, secrets, or URLs                    | Security violation                                      |
| Unvalidated external input at any boundary              | Security and runtime stability risk                     |
| New Queue instantiation outside `@xtanbot/queues`       | Fragmented job infrastructure                           |
| Calling `runAgent` outside `@xtanbot/ai-core` consumers | Bypasses kernel, duplicates orchestration logic         |
| Redis keys without TTL                                  | Memory leak, operational incident                       |
| Storing secrets, PII, or transcripts in logs            | Privacy violation, regulatory risk                      |
| Full audio buffer before TTS streaming                  | Latency violation, unacceptable voice UX                |
| Inline prompt strings in application code               | Unversioned, untestable, ungovernanced                  |
| Skipping Twilio signature validation                    | Security vulnerability, enables spoofed telephony       |
| Skipping rate limiting on call/AI initiation            | Abuse vector, catastrophic cost risk                    |
| Hard deleting user-owned entities                       | Data loss, violates audit and compliance requirements   |
| TypeScript `enum` declarations                          | Use `z.enum()` or `as const` instead                    |
| Non-null assertion `!` in non-test code                 | Runtime crash vector                                    |
| Unhandled promise rejections                            | Silent failure, undefined system state                  |
| Domain events without a Zod schema                      | Untyped contracts, runtime surprises                    |
| Metrics defined outside `@xtanbot/observability`        | Fragmented observability, invisible system behavior     |


